# Revival Archive

<h3 class="mercury-project">
	a <img src="/logos/mercury.svg" alt="" class="logo" /> <a href="https://mercs.dev">Mercury 3</a> project
</h3>

<div in:fade|global class="content">
Welcome to the Revival Archive, a website dedicated to keeping track of active/defunct old Roblox private servers.

Check out our [blog posts](/blog), [reviews](/reviews) and most importantly, the [revival index](/revivals).

---

## What is a "revival" / "private server"?

A "revival" or a private server aims to recreate or bring back the experience of Roblox.
These use specific clients, depending on what year of Roblox they would like to focus on (e.g. 2014). 
Almost all revivals have a website where the user can socialize with other members of the community, etc. 
Typically, Discord is favoured in terms of communication but some revivals can have an active forum.  

Some of these projects are private and may require invitation or chosen access to join, these tend to be website-based projects and usually the reason behind this is to control the number of people joining at once or importantly for security (allowing trusted members only).

## How can I join a revival?

You can easily find a revival perfect for you on the Revival Archive!

## I have my own revival, how can I put it on the Revival Archive?

You can go ahead and join the official Mercury Core [Discord](https://discord.gg/W9dJk9Htnq) server - there you can request for your/somebody else's revival to be added to the archive! 

Since we are also open-source, you can also add it yourself! See below:

## How can I contribute to the Revival Archive?

The Revival Archive is an open-source community-first project. Visit our [Github](https://github.com/tp-link-extender/revival-archive), fork the code and start writing your own pages!

Create a pull request with the main repository and the maintainers will merge your changes as soon as possible.

---

We are not affiliated with Roblox Corporation.
